\subsection{Look up an institution by unique ID}\label{LookUpByID}

LADOK and similar handlers should not hard-code SeamlessAccess URLs
throughout their login logic.
This wrapper keeps that dependency in one place and normalizes a bad
response into a single exception shape that callers can treat as a
lookup failure.

<<SeamlessAccess lookup functions>>=
def get_entity_data_by_id(id):
  """
  Requests entity data from SeamlessAccess.org for entity with unique ID `id`.
  Returns JSON (dictionary) containing data.
  """
  response = requests.get(f"https://md.seamlessaccess.org/entities/{id}.json")
  try:
    return response.json()
  except Exception:
    raise Exception(f"invalid response from SeamlessAccess.org for ID {id}")
@

We can test this functions as follows:
<<test functions>>=
def test_get_entity_data_by_id():
  data = get_entity_data_by_id("{sha1}e26e5d098f073536d0351577c98c83825f0f922c")
  assert data["id"] == "{sha1}e26e5d098f073536d0351577c98c83825f0f922c"
@

The resulting JSON data for this particular ID is:
\begin{pycode}
import json
from weblogin.seamlessaccess import *

print(r"\begin{minted}{json}")
print(json.dumps(
  get_entity_data_by_id("{sha1}e26e5d098f073536d0351577c98c83825f0f922c"),
  indent=2
))
print(r"\end{minted}")
\end{pycode}

\subsection{Searching for an institution by name}

Name-based lookup has the same purpose, but returns a list because
SeamlessAccess can match several institutions.
The wrapper keeps that API detail local so higher-level handlers can
decide whether to pick the first match or reject ambiguity.

<<SeamlessAccess lookup functions>>=
def find_entity_data_by_name(name):
  """
  Searches SeamlessAccess.org for an institution by name `name`.
  Returns a list of institutions' data.
  """
  response = requests.get(f"https://md.seamlessaccess.org/entities/?q={name}")
  try:
    return response.json()
  except Exception:
    raise Exception(f"invalid response from SeamlessAccess.org for name {name}")
@

We can test this functions as follows:
<<test functions>>=
def test_find_entity_data_by_name():
  data = find_entity_data_by_name("KTH")
  assert data[0]["id"] == "{sha1}e26e5d098f073536d0351577c98c83825f0f922c"
@

The resulting JSON data for this particular ID is the following.
Note that the difference compared to the data in \cref{LookUpByID} is that this
is one element in a list.
\begin{pycode}
import json
from weblogin.seamlessaccess import *

print(r"\begin{minted}{json}")
print(json.dumps(
  find_entity_data_by_name("KTH"),
  indent=2
))
print(r"\end{minted}")
\end{pycode}

\subsection{Resolving an institution to its entity ID}

The higher-level handlers ([[weblogin.ladok.SSOlogin]], for instance)
don't care about the raw entity data: what they need is the
institution's [[entityID]], the URL identifying the institution's SAML
identity provider.
They also let the user identify the institution either by its unique
ID or by a search string.
The dispatch between the two lookups above is thus the same for every
handler, so it belongs here rather than in each of them.

An ID always begins with its hash-algorithm prefix, such as
[[{sha1}]], so we dispatch on that prefix.
An earlier version checked whether [[{sha1}]] occurred \emph{anywhere}
in the string, which would misdirect a search string that merely
mentions the prefix to the ID lookup.

A name search may match several institutions, and we cannot tell which
one the user meant.
We warn, list the candidates and default to the first match: this
keeps names convenient for interactive use, while the listed IDs in
the warning show the user how to make the choice unambiguous.
<<SeamlessAccess lookup functions>>=
def find_entity_id(institution):
  """
  Resolves `institution` to the entity ID (the SAML IdP URL)
  registered with SeamlessAccess.org.

  `institution` is either a unique ID (starting with a hash prefix
  such as `{sha1}`) or a search string, for example the institution's
  name.

  Raises ValueError if no institution matches. If several match,
  warns and uses the first.
  """
  if institution.startswith("{sha1}"):
    data = get_entity_data_by_id(institution)
  else:
    <<let [[data]] be the best match for the searched institution>>

  try:
    return data["entityID"]
  except KeyError:
    raise Exception(
      f"SeamlessAccess.org returned unexpected result: {data}")
@

<<let [[data]] be the best match for the searched institution>>=
matches = find_entity_data_by_name(institution)
if not matches:
  raise ValueError(f"{institution} didn't give any match.")
if len(matches) > 1:
  logger.warning("Several institutions match %s, using the first:",
                 institution)
  for match in matches:
    logger.warning("* %s (%s)", match.get("title"), match.get("id"))
  logger.warning("Use the institution's ID instead for precision.")
data = matches[0]
@

We test the resolution without network access by replacing the two
lookup functions.
First the dispatch: an ID must go to the ID lookup---we make the name
search fail the test if it's ever consulted---and a name to the name
search.
<<test functions>>=
def test_find_entity_id_dispatches_on_id_prefix(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(sa, "get_entity_data_by_id",
    lambda institution: {"entityID": "https://idp.a.example.com"})
  monkeypatch.setattr(sa, "find_entity_data_by_name",
    lambda institution: pytest.fail("searched by name"))
  assert sa.find_entity_id("{sha1}0123abc") == "https://idp.a.example.com"

def test_find_entity_id_by_name(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(sa, "find_entity_data_by_name",
    lambda institution: [{"title": "A University",
                          "id": "{sha1}aaa",
                          "entityID": "https://idp.a.example.com"}])
  assert sa.find_entity_id("A University") == "https://idp.a.example.com"
@

Then the ambiguous and empty search results.
<<test functions>>=
def test_find_entity_id_ambiguous_warns(monkeypatch, caplog):
  monkeypatch.setattr(sa, "find_entity_data_by_name",
    lambda institution: [{"title": "A University",
                          "id": "{sha1}aaa",
                          "entityID": "https://idp.a.example.com"},
                         {"title": "B University",
                          "id": "{sha1}bbb",
                          "entityID": "https://idp.b.example.com"}])
  assert sa.find_entity_id("University") == "https://idp.a.example.com"
  assert "Several institutions match" in caplog.text
  assert "{sha1}bbb" in caplog.text

def test_find_entity_id_no_match(monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr(sa, "find_entity_data_by_name",
    lambda institution: [])
  with pytest.raises(ValueError):
    sa.find_entity_id("Nonexistent University")
